The instruction-count kit's changes to QEMU 11.1.2's user-mode emulator (sdk/insncap): the guest
program runs, and counts, the same every time, and it can't touch the emulator's own memory. The
guest's system calls, its clocks and its randomness among them, are insncap.c's to answer.

1. The time-stamp counter (rdtsc, and rdtscp, which reads it too) advances by 1024 on every read
   instead of following the host's clock, and TSC_AUX (rdtscp's ECX, and rdpid) is 0 instead of
   the host CPU the emulator happens to run on.
2. Under -R (a guest address space reserved below 4 GiB, laid out by QEMU alone, so the same in
   every run whatever the host's own mappings), x86-64's vsyscall page, at the top of the address
   space, is out of range, and QEMU refuses to start. The guest goes without it: a call there
   faults like any unmapped address (C libraries use the vDSO, whose calls are system calls).
3. Every guest load and store is range-checked before it happens. In user mode a guest address
   becomes the host's `guest_base + address`, unchecked, so a guest that can make a pointer (C,
   unsafe Rust, Python's ctypes or numpy's `__array_interface__`) could read or write anything
   in the emulator: its code buffer, its data, the plugin's count. Now:
   - translated code (tcg/x86_64, prepare_host_addr's user-mode branch) compares each access's
     address with the last valid guest address less its size, and sends one past it to the slow
     path, two host instructions per access;
   - the slow path and every helper's access (cpu_mmu_lookup: the ld/st helpers, cpu_ld*/cpu_st*)
     and every atomic (atomic_mmu_lookup: cmpxchg, cmpxchg16b, the locked read-modify-writes)
     check the range again, and an access outside it ends the process with status 135 at once,
     before anything of it lands (the kit reports it as `escape`); the guest can't catch it;
   - tlb_vaddr_to_host gives no host pointer outside the range.
   Instruction fetches (probe_access_internal) and system calls (access_ok, guest_range_valid)
   already check the range; sdk/insncap/README.md lists every host-address conversion and what
   covers it.
